{"id": "210fb8a3-6c63-401b-ab64-3e68563137ce", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:48:41.982789", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "call_c50523eef3934f08be0f77de", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "# Independent verification brief: exagium `probe/`, `counter/`, `record/` and `loop/`\n\nYou are an independent verifier. Someone else built this tool. Your job is to check its claims and try to make it report something false. Don't trust the README; report only what you can show with commands, numbers and line references. A result of \"I couldn't break X\" is useful, and so is \"X is wrong, here is how\".\n\n**The most valuable thing you can do is run it on hardware the author never had.** It was built and checked on one 2-vCPU Intel cloud VM with no energy meters. Examples:\n- bare metal;\n- an AMD or ARM CPU;\n- a machine with readable RAPL (`/sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl*`) or working `perf` counters;\n- an NVIDIA GPU host.\n\n## Setup\n```\ngit clone https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium && cd exagium/probe\npython3 -m unittest -v test_probe          # expect 40 passing, ~65 s\npython3 probe.py --quick --out quick.json  # ~10 s\npython3 probe.py --out full.json           # ~100 s on 2 cores; longer with more cores or RAM\npython3 probe.py --reanalyse full.json; echo $?\n```\nNeeds Linux, a C compiler and Python 3 (stdlib only). Valgrind is optional.\n\n**Rules:**\n- Run only one measurement process at a time, and nothing heavy alongside it, because it corrupts timing.\n- Record your exact hardware (`lscpu`, `nproc`, RAM, VM or bare metal, kernel) in your report.\n\n## Claims to test\nGive each a verdict: CONFIRMED / REFUTED / UNCLEAR, with evidence.\n\n1. **Compute ceilings are real FMA throughput.**\n   - Check this with `objdump -d` on a build of `kernels.c` (`-O2 -march=native`): `scalar_f64` should be scalar FMAs with no auto-vectorisation, and `vec_f64`/`vec_f32` should be full-width vector FMAs with enough independent chains to be throughput-bound.\n   - Compare the measured GFLOP/s against your CPU's theoretical peak: cores × clock × FMA units × lanes × 2. Find out your clock under load yourself.\n   - Check the FLOP formula in the code.\n2. **Bandwidth.**\n   - The L1 figure should be load-bound, not limited by the add chain. Try changing the number of accumulators.\n   - The DRAM figure should be a genuine DRAM read rate. Compare with an independent tool if you can (e.g. `mbw`, STREAM, Intel MLC, likwid).\n3. **Latency curve.** The pointer chase is one random cycle over cache lines, can't be optimised away, and reports ns per dependent load. Compare with an independent tool if you can (e.g. lmbench `lat_mem_rd`, Intel MLC).\n4. **Memory levels (derived).**\n   - The tool claims it assumes no cache sizes, excludes points that break monotonicity as interference, and reports a level only where the rise across it is small next to the jump at each edge. When no next level is found, the bracket's upper end must be `null`, never \"end of sweep\".\n   - Compare its levels and capacity brackets with your CPU's documented caches and with an independent latency tool.\n   - Claimed simulation error rates are in the table in `probe/README.md`. Mostly they are 0% phantom levels, 0% false boundaries and 0 wrong brackets, apart from a stated limit with interference spikes on a level's last point.\n   - A previous verifier's adversarial cases and noisy real curve are now regression tests, so try something new.\n   - Reproduce those rates with **your own** synthetic generator, not the one in `test_probe.py`. Then try to make the fit lie with a new adversarial curve, calling `probe.describe_levels(sizes, medians, ses, df=reps-1, rep_max=..., rep_min=...)` directly. Report any curve that yields a phantom level, a false boundary, or a wrong bracket in more than 5% of noisy draws.\n5. **Meter tiers.**\n   - Each tier is meant to be detected by trying it, not by checking whether a binary exists.\n   - On your machine, check every tier's `available` value and evidence against reality.\n   - If you can, change the conditions and confirm the tier flips: install or remove `perf`, change `/proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid`, use a GPU host, make RAPL readable.\n6. **`--reanalyse`.**\n   - It claims to recompute every statistic from raw reps, and to check rep counts, rate = work ÷ time, chase seeds and, on the same host, the meter tiers.\n   - It exits 0 when consistent, 1 when not, and 2 when consistent but run on another host.\n   - It explicitly does **not** claim to prove the raw data is genuine.\n   - Find a tamper it misses that would let someone overstate a result, and say whether it matters.\n7. **Labels.** Every number in the profile should be marked measured / reported / derived, or be plainly raw or settings. List anything that could be mistaken for a measurement.\n8. **Reproducibility.** Run the full probe 3 times with different `--seed` values. Do the levels, brackets and ceilings agree within the reported noise? Is the run-to-run spread larger than the within-run SE suggests?\n9. **Portability.** What breaks on your platform (ARM, AMD, other compilers, containers, macOS)? Does anything fail silently or quietly report a wrong number instead of failing loudly?\n\n## Counter claims (`counter/`)\n```\ncd exagium/counter\npython3 -m unittest -v test_count     # expect 16 passing, ~16 s; needs valgrind >= 3.22\ngcc -O2 -o workloads/mlp workloads/mlp.c && sha256sum workloads/mlp\ncd workloads && for n in 1000 0; do python3 ../count.py --input mlp -- ./mlp $n; done\npython3 ../count.py --check ../../runs/<record>.json\n```\n10. **Repeatability.** Reps of a deterministic program are identical; with the default environment, pure Python is too. Find a deterministic program whose reps differ, or a nondeterministic one reported as `exact`.\n11. **Cross-host.** The README claims the CPU never changes the counts, and that the program's own work (`Ir` for `./mlp 1000` minus `./mlp 0`) is identical on every host of an architecture for the same binary: 35,765,911 on x86-64 (sha256 `a330b376…`) and 30,898,727 on arm64. Report your sha256, both totals, your OS image and CPU. If they differ with the same binary, say what differs (`cg_diff`, `--keep-raw`).\n12. **Isolation.** The command runs in a fresh fixed-path sandbox with a fixed environment, unaffected by `.valgrindrc`, `VALGRIND_OPTS` or the caller; children are counted; code holding valgrind client requests is flagged. Find a way for a candidate to do work that isn't counted, or to make its counts look smaller than its real work, while the counter exits 0. (Known and stated: absolute paths outside the sandbox, other processes, dlopen/JIT code.)\n13. **`--check`.** It should exit 0 for a genuine record on the same software, 1 for edited or forged counts, and 2 when it can't compare like for like. Find a tamper that passes.\n14. **Simulated vs measured.** Run `experiments/sweep.py` against your own probe profile and compare the simulated levels with your measured curve.\n\n## Record claims (`record/`)\nThe ledger's whole point is that nobody, including its owner, can lie in it without a stranger noticing. Read `record/README.md`, then try to make it lie. Setup: a full clone (not `--depth 1`), and `pip install sigstore==4.5.1 opentimestamps-client==0.8.0` in a virtualenv.\n```\ncd exagium && python3 record/record.py verify && python3 record/record.py check-anchors && python3 record/record.py monitor\n```\n1. **Run the monitor yourself, now and later.** Every Rekor attestation by `record.yml` on any ref must be in the ledger, or be published by an orphan entry. This is most useful done repeatedly, from outside the owner's control. Report each run's output and date.\n2. **Write an entry nobody can tell from a genuine one.** The owner controls the repo, the inbox and the code. Try hand-written entries, rewritten history, deleted anchors, orphan laundering, and code changes that `writer_code_changed` misses. Earlier rounds' attacks are listed in the card; find new ones.\n3. **Verify an anchor without `record.py`:** use the card's `sigstore` and `gh` commands, and OpenTimestamps through a node or opentimestamps.org. Recompute an entry's sha256 in another language, since entries are RFC 8785 (JCS) bytes.\n4. **Exit codes.** Wrong data must exit 1, never 3. \"Can't check\" (exit 2) must never hide a wrong anchor.\n\n## Loop claims (`loop/`)\nThe first loop scores small digit classifiers against two mandatory baselines: accuracy, simulated instructions per image (startup included) and native time. Ledger entry 2 is a pre-registered plan about three MLPs (`loop/candidates/`). Read `loop/README.md` and `loop.py`'s docstring. Needs Linux, gcc, valgrind 3.22+, bubblewrap (unprivileged user namespaces allowed), `numpy==2.5.3`, and the record's virtualenv.\n```\npython3 loop/loop.py reproduce loop/baselines/* loop/candidates/*      # weights must retrain byte for byte\npython3 loop/loop.py evaluate loop/baselines/linear loop/baselines/thermo loop/candidates/mlp16 \\\n  loop/candidates/mlp32 loop/candidates/mlp64 --test test --plan 2 --rounds 30 --seed 20261106\n```\n1. **Re-measure plan 2 on your hardware** and judge it by its own decision rule. Accuracy and predictions should match ours exactly; Ir/img should match ours on the same architecture.\n2. **Read every `train.py` and `infer.c`.** The test labels are public, and counts are a claim for cooperative code (the docstring says why). Does any candidate carry test data in, or take a different path under valgrind?\n3. **Make a submission score better than it earns,** or make a failed check read as success. Earlier rounds' findings are listed in the card.\n\n## Report\nKeep it under about 800 words, in this order:\n1. Your hardware.\n2. The verdict table.\n3. Evidence per claim.\n4. Every way you found that the tool could report something false, ranked by how badly, each with a concrete reproduction.\n\nAttach your `full.json` profiles if you can. If you have a GitHub account, an issue on the repository with the report is ideal.\n# record — spec card\n\n**What it is.** The third brick: an append-only, hash-chained ledger of **plans** (a claim and the test fixed before it runs) and **verdicts** (the outcome, given by someone other than the claimant).\n- **One writer.** Only `.github/workflows/record.yml` writes to it. Each run attests the exact range of entries it added: a Sigstore attestation in the public Rekor log, plus an OpenTimestamps proof in Bitcoin.\n- **A stranger's checks:**\n  - every entry is covered by the writer's attestations, with no gap;\n  - every anchor is genuine;\n  - every attestation the writer ever made, on any ref GitHub names, is in the ledger, or is explained by an orphan entry that publishes what it attested.\n- **Why these anchors:** `experiments/anchors/RESULTS.md`, attacked by an outside verifier (`experiments/anchors/verifier1/`).\n\n**Run.**\n```\npython3 record.py verify          # chain intact? also lists open and overdue plans\npython3 record.py head            # head seq and sha256\npython3 record.py check-anchors   # every anchor right; writer's ranges cover 1..head; each run's commit\npython3 record.py monitor         # every Rekor attestation by record.yml on any ref, vs the ledger\npython3 -m unittest -v test_record   # 20 tests, <1 s\n```\n- `verify` and `head` need only Python 3 (stdlib).\n- `check-anchors` needs `sigstore==4.5.1` and `opentimestamps-client==0.8.0` (use a virtualenv), plus network access to blockstream.info and mempool.space.\n- `monitor` needs network access to the GitHub API and Rekor. To judge orphan entries it needs a full (not shallow) git clone of the ledger branch; a shallow clone exits 2.\n\n**Exit codes:**\n- **0:** ok.\n- **1:** something is wrong:\n  - a ledger rule is broken;\n  - an anchor is wrong;\n  - the writer's ranges leave a gap or overlap, or don't reach the head;\n  - an attestation is unexplained, or an orphan explanation is refused;\n  - the ledger branch was force-pushed, or deleted (and recreated).\n- **2:** can't check (missing library, network). `check-anchors` checks every anchor before deciding, so a problem in one doesn't hide a problem in another.\n- **3:** unexpected failure. Wrong data never exits 3; a failure is never reported as a verdict.\n\n## How it's written\nProposals go in `ledger/inbox/NAME.json` as `{\"kind\": \"plan\" | \"verdict\" | \"orphan\", \"body\": {...}}`. A push to `main` that touches the inbox runs `record.yml`, which:\n1. **Runs exactly the commit that triggered it,** the one its certificate will name. If `main` has already moved on, it steps aside. The newer push's run, or failing that the daily run, takes the proposals. One writer runs at a time across all refs.\n2. **Refuses to add anything unless the writer's earlier attestations cover every existing entry,** so an entry written by hand is never vouched for later.\n3. **Ingests the proposals,** stamps the new head with OpenTimestamps, and refuses to attest if `main` has moved.\n4. **Attests the range it added:** predicate `{from_seq, to_seq, prev_sha256, head_sha256}`, with the head entry as subject. Then it checks every anchor and commits.\n5. **If it fails or is cancelled after attesting,** it pushes the bundle and the entries to the `ci-runs` branch (`orphans/<run>/`), so the orphaned attestation can be explained with its real statement and entries. That branch is owner-writable, so it's a convenience, not evidence.\n\nThe daily run also completes pending OpenTimestamps proofs.\n- Steps run under `bash -eo pipefail`, so a failing command in a pipe fails the run.\n- `record.py` runs under `python3 -I`, so a file next to it can't shadow the standard library.\n- `checkout` and `attest` are pinned by commit SHA.\n- The pip packages are pinned by version, not by hash.\n\n**Entries.** `ledger/entries/NNNNNN.json`: `{seq, prev, kind, written_at, body}`.\n- **An entry's identity** is the sha256 of its stored bytes, and `prev` is the previous entry's.\n- **The bytes must be exactly RFC 8785 (JCS)** of the entry, with no trailing newline, so any language can recompute them. Floats are refused (write them as strings). Keys must be ASCII, integers within ±(2⁵³−1), and lone surrogates are refused. Under these rules Python's `json.dumps(sort_keys, separators, ensure_ascii=False)` equals JCS. Fuzzed against the `rfc8785` library: 0 mismatches in 20,000 random objects (a reviewer repeated it); the test runs 3,000.\n- **Duplicate keys,** non-canonical bytes, stray files, symlinks and directories are refused.\n- **`written_at` is the writer's clock,** a claim. The anchors say when an entry really existed.\n\n**Rules `verify` enforces:**\n- seq runs 1, 2, 3…, and `prev` links each entry to the one before;\n- body fields are known and well-typed;\n- `by` is a lowercase ASCII handle, so look-alike names can't pass as different people;\n- `verdict_due` must be a real date;\n- a verdict names an earlier plan by seq *and* sha256;\n- **one verdict per plan:** a re-run needs a new plan, so re-running until a result looks good shows up as several plans;\n{\n    \"mediaType\": \"application/vnd.dev.sigstore.bundle.v0.3+json\",\n    \"verificationMaterial\": {\n        \"certificate\": {\n            \"rawBytes\": \"\"\n        },\n        \"tlogEntries\": [\n            {\n                \"logIndex\": \"3711043444\",\n                \"logId\": {\n                    \"keyId\": \"wNI9atQGlz+VWfO6LRygH4QUfY/8W4RFwiT5i5WRgB0=\"\n                },\n                \"kindVersion\": {\n                    \"kind\": \"dsse\",\n                    \"version\": \"0.0.1\"\n                },\n                \"integratedTime\": \"1793945388\",\n                \"inclusionPromise\": {\n                    \"signedEntryTimestamp\": \"MEUCIQCu0wfvqvJHXj64Uy5kAHqsx+X0DvE4M1H2Yg3rotaiAQIgE2RJJW/wg59knoLaSIbNOvYyJnf4TGlstPGKZCQG/9A=\"\n                },\n                \"inclusionProof\": {\n                    \"logIndex\": \"3589139182\",\n                    \"rootHash\": \"8xks7VTYbJKVoS15JUGPMVNh3x6diu4sO64X1kTcK8c=\",\n                    \"treeSize\": \"3589139559\",\n                    \"hashes\": [\n                        \"iwYQj/g7V8irk4XNkhzFmOuKq9IasKBttK9mQiEzIoE=\",\n                        \"i4njiJW/kELmgwIoF/lQh2y6y7I6PleSC63DBw+oSLw=\",\n                        \"gOfrWjdIsmP6Q0fHlG320J25xqOmUUriyXF9+cHHF4c=\",\n                        \"SckNEv8hJzOw30hJQf86p2FW974YmIkuvewMMWAkElU=\",\n                        \"917yV+R5K6huuXaHE5QmbpsvEnI9k8r0dfKDbY9RazU=\",\n                        \"8l24ODPNT4GqsWnXVnSyLM+36uppaCDOq1tE3KUQymY=\",\n                        \"8/9KuzQZ1rwtls23nSSHZTFVtDVAFo6Ckm6nPjZRlN0=\",\n                        \"buly6Z4UF21JYb4t4Bb4iT5S6rG53Pxm/wLOXZQPztY=\",\n                        \"gdL+hm/GVtbCo2GeHpazclFjDv4EfJ5IumUcdp+j+AQ=\",\n                        \"8kWJsW2PrGud34eH/L14hrJ3fFotT7xKKoLXdhm4lw8=\",\n                        \"WlrDfWfDBPYyTIJFpEuafEQniLeDSjmSsaN6FTszdKg=\",\n                        \"kh6CqnNZajdQ2jx46XWreRPygYssCbAnhT2cbNqePMA=\",\n                        \"+1F4fwBzQbC7UToV1pD6LZwPk9E3yCpHwtFUzmiISoQ=\",\n                        \"mNfV6ypLq6f2j8bpvbhnzTt/a8Xezop6tuuQOWuSYpk=\",\n                        \"vhB9Y8uw/Gw04Ij0U3bSwgB0JHEEDt5PsZ4yQ8rkqV0=\",\n{\"body\":{\"baseline\":\"loop/baselines/linear (logistic regression on pixels/16) and loop/baselines/thermo (thermometer encoding + logistic regression, run as a 64x17x10 lookup table), unmodified.\",\"by\":\"claude-loop1-proposer\",\"claim\":\"On the optdigits test set (1,797 images, 13 unseen writers), (A) mlp16, a 64-16-10 ReLU MLP run as int16 per-pixel lookup tables, costs less than half the simulated in\n# Verifier brief: what actually anchors an honest record?\n\nYou're an independent verifier for exagium (https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium, public). Don't trust the author. Your job:\n- find out which outside anchor does the job described below, or whether any does;\n- follow the question down to its base, even if the answer is \"none of these\", \"a different design\", or \"an anchor isn't the real fix\".\n\nReport what you measured, not what you expect.\n\n## The job\nexagium records claims about measurements and models, together with the verdict on each claim. The owner's rule is that **results can never be lied about**. The record is an append-only log in a public git repo, written by CI. An *anchor* is something outside the owner's control that a stranger can check.\n\nTreat the owner, and anyone holding the repo or the CI token, as the adversary. A good anchor lets a stranger with no trust in the owner check each of these:\n\n| # | Requirement | Question a stranger must be able to answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | Existence by time | Did record D exist no later than time T? |\n| R2 | No backdating | Could the owner have made D after T and claimed T? |\n| R3 | No silent edit/removal | If D was changed or deleted later, will I notice? |\n| R4 | Completeness | Am I seeing *every* record, including the unfavourable ones? |\n| R5 | No equivocation | Does everyone see the same history? |\n| R6 | Longevity | Will this still verify in 10–20 years, if the provider dies or its certs expire? |\n| R7 | Practical | Is it free, automatable from CI and VMs, free of personal names, checkable from a phone, and verifiable in another language? |\n\n**R4 and R5 are the hard part.** The author's own finding is that every route tested proves existence, R1 to R3. None proves completeness. A hash chain doesn't help against an owner who keeps two chains, runs privately, or re-runs until a result is good. Find out what does, if anything.\n\n## What's already measured (reproduce it, then break it)\n- **Results:** `record/experiments/anchors/RESULTS.md` on branch `anchor-test`.\n- **Script:** `try_anchors.sh`. **Workflow:** `.github/workflows/anchor-test.yml`.\n- **Raw results:** branch `ci-runs`, `anchors/<run_id>/`. They include the RFC 3161 tokens (`r*.tsr`), the query, `digest.txt`, OTS proofs and the Sigstore bundle (`attestation.jsonl`).\n- **The digest anchored everywhere:** `fe8d93a9dfb783350aa23a0c4471020a1b6cf9a17f6e5bd317b748e045b31de8`, the sha256 of `runs/count-2026-11-05T073638Z-0feb5e325c25.json` on `main`.\n\nThe author's claims, in short:\n- **Repo and gist:** both under one owner, so no independence. The gist is the weaker of the two: it has no activity log, and CI's token gets a 403 when writing one.\n- **RFC 3161:** six free TSAs grant and verify. A token fails after its certificate expires unless re-stamped.\n- **Sigstore:** the attestation sits in public Rekor, with the identity bound to repo, workflow, ref and commit. sigstore-python verifies it offline.\n- **OpenTimestamps:** works, but takes hours to confirm and needs Bitcoin data to verify.\n- **GH Archive and Software Heritage:** neither holds anything of ours.\n\nThe proposed next design, a hypothesis you should test: a hash-chained log in git, with CI as its only writer. Each chain head gets a Sigstore attestation plus tokens from two RFC 3161 TSAs.\n\n## Tasks\n**1. Reproduce.** Verify the committed tokens, OTS proofs and Sigstore bundle on your machine, without trusting the author's verify output.\n- Record exact commands and tool versions.\n- The OTS proofs were pending at commit time; they should be complete by now. Upgrade and verify them, and say how: a node, block headers or an explorer.\n\n**2. Attack each route as a dishonest owner.** Use your own throwaway public repo or fork, since you can't push to exagium. For every route, try to:\n- **a. Backdate:** make a record now and get the anchor to say an earlier time.\n- **b. Remove or edit:** drop an unfavourable record and change another so a stranger doesn't notice. Include: force push, deleting and recreating the repo, deleting a GitHub attestation through the API, deleting a gist, not showing a token.\n- **c. Equivocate:** keep two histories and anchor both. Can a stranger find out?\n- **d. Withhold:** run privately, or re-run until good, and anchor only the good run. Can a stranger find out?\n- **e. Abuse the identity:** can another workflow, branch or fork produce an attestation that passes a verifier who checks only the repo?\n\nScore each route against R1–R7: **pass**, **fail** or **conditional**, with the condition. Give the evidence for each.\n\n**3. Dig to the base.** For R4 and R5, research and test, where feasible, what actually gives completeness and non-equivocation. Candidates are below; add any we missed.\n- **Transparency logs with witness cosigning:** Certificate Transparency's gossip, sigsum, transparency-dev witnesses, Rekor monitors such as rekor-monitor watching a workflow identity. Can a stranger list *every* Rekor entry for one workflow identity? How?\n- **Pre-registration:** anchor the test plan before the run, so a plan without a published verdict is visible. Does that close withholding? What about re-runs?\n- **CI as the only writer,** with branch protection, required workflows and the activity log. What can the repo owner still do?\n- **Independent archives as witnesses:** Software Heritage \"save code now\", Zenodo deposits, Internet Archive.\n- **Bitcoin directly,** versus via OpenTimestamps.\n- **Reframing.** Maybe R4 can't be had from an anchor at all, and the real defence is independent re-measurement: anyone re-runs the pre-registered test and gets the same verdict. Is that enough, and what remains?\n\n**4. Longevity and practice.**\n- Test RFC 3161 tokens with `openssl ts -verify` and `-attime` past the cert's expiry.\n- Find what re-stamping (RFC 4998 evidence records) would take.\n- Check terms of service and rate limits for the free TSAs and for Sigstore's public instance, including the move from Rekor v1 to v2.\n- Check whether each route can be verified from a phone browser.\n- Check how a non-Python verifier would recompute a record digest. Python's `json.dumps(sort_keys)` isn't a cross-language canonical form; RFC 8785 JCS is the candidate.\n\n## Rules\n- Don't spam free services: at most about 20 requests to any one TSA or calendar in total.\n- Put nothing personal in what you anchor. Use digests of test files only.\n- Don't push to exagium, and don't open issues there.\n- If a claim of the author's is wrong, say exactly which one and show it.\n- If you can't test something, say so and why. Don't infer a pass.\n\n## Report (return as a zip)\n- `report.md`:\n  - a requirement × route table (pass, fail or conditional, with evidence);\n  - your attacks and their outcomes;"}], "is_error": false, "command": "cd /home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/ws && cat VERIFY.md && head -60 record/README.md && python3 -m json.tool ledger/anchors/000002.sigstore.json | head -40 && head -c 400 ledger/entries/000002.json; echo; cat record/experiments/anchors/VERIFY-anchors.md | head -80", "exit_code": 0, "timeout": false, "metadata": {"exit_code": 0, "pid": -1, "username": "runner", "hostname": "runnervm7aw5e", "working_dir": "/home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/ws", "py_interpreter_path": "/usr/bin/python", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"}, "full_output_save_dir": "/home/runner/.openhands/conversations/781729e6787e4a99b4c609057030b3ac/observations", "kind": "TerminalObservation"}, "action_id": "13c0b0f6-fc64-493e-b5dd-91a3b1148375", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
