{"id": "e86bda12-f5cb-4b4e-90fe-36db02e1c344", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:49:44.420203", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the Sigstore attestation for entry 2 has range from_seq=2, to_seq=2. So an .ots proof for a range head — the .ots file corresponding to `to_seq` (e.g. `000002.ots` covers entry 2). The bug: when an attacker deletes `anchors/000002.ots` (the OpenTimestamps proof for range head 2), `check-anchors` should flag it. Currently what happens?\n\nIn `cmd_check_anchors`, `_listdir` on anchors lists files. ANCHOR_NAME regex: `([0-9]{6})\\.(sigstore\\.json|ots)` fullmatch. If the .ots file is deleted, the listdir just won't include it. Then `covered` is computed from sigstore ranges only — coverage comes from Sigstore ranges, not from ots files. And `covered` = max hi from ranges; with entries 1..4 covered by ranges... ranges probably: run 1 wrote entry 1 (from 1 to 1), run 2 wrote entry 2 (from 2 to 2), etc. Actually from the predicate: run for entry 2 attests range from_seq 2 to_seq 2, meaning the first entry (from_seq 1) was attested by an earlier run whose head was entry 1... whatever.\n\nKey issue: when the .ots file is missing, check-anchors silently skips it (listdir just doesn't list it; also a *stray* file would be bad, but a missing one is not detected). The required behaviour: exit 1 with stderr message containing `NNNNNN.ots is missing`, e.g. `000002.ots is missing`.\n\nAlso, the coverage logic: `covered` from sigstore ranges. If an .ots for the head is deleted, covered stays fine because ranges come from sigstore bundles which are still there. But required behavior: check-anchors must exit 1 when the .ots proof for any attested range head is missing.\n\nDesign of fix: For each Sigstore anchor with attested range (from_seq, to_seq), there should be a corresponding `.ots` proof at `anchors/{to_seq:06d}.ots` (the head of the range — per workflow: each run stamps the head it added, i.e. the head of the range added; predicate from_seq = before+1, to_seq = new head seq; the ots proof is for the head entry, which is the last entry of the range). So for each sigstore anchor with to_seq = N, expect anchors/NNNNNN.ots to exist.\n\nCurrently the loop iterates over anchors dir; for ots files it calls check_ots; for sigstore it verifies and appends range. Missing .ots files are never noticed.\n\nFix approach: in cmd_check_anchors, after collecting ranges (or in the anchor loop), check that the expected .ots file for each attested range head exists; if missing, add to `bad` a message like f\"anchors/{seq:06d}.ots is missing\" — must contain `NNNNNN.ots is missing`, e.g. \"000002.ots is missing\". The stderr print uses f\"BAD: {x}\". The test will presumably check stderr contains \"000002.ots is missing\". Let me make bad message like f\"anchors/{m.group(0)}.ots is missing\"? Simpler: f\"{seq:06d}.ots is missing\".\n\nWait — required message: \"It must print a message to stderr containing `NNNNNN.ots is missing` (for example `000002.ots is missing`).\" So the stderr output (which goes through `print(f\"BAD: {x}\", file=sys.stderr)`) should contain that substring. So the bad string should be e.g. `anchors/000002.ots is missing` or just `000002.ots is missing`.\n\nNow, how do tests test this? Existing tests: `test_check_anchors_requires_the_writer_to_cover_every_entry` runs check-anchors with empty ledger (no anchors at all) and expects rc 1 with \"never attested by the writer\". With empty anchors dir and empty chain? Actually _two_entries gives 2 entries, no anchors → ranges empty, covered=0 < 2 → bad. Good.\n\nA new hidden test probably: create ledger with entries, add a sigstore anchor... but sigstore verification requires the library + network. Hmm. Hidden tests must run offline. How would a test create an attested anchor? Maybe via monkeypatching: e.g. R.check_sigstore patched? The task says \"All existing tests in record/test_record.py must still pass\" and the hidden test will test the new behaviour.\n\nThe hidden test likely does something like: run check-anchors on a ledger where a sigstore anchor exists... hard without lib. More likely the test patches. But we can't know. Let's design robustly:\n\nIn cmd_check_anchors, for each anchor file:\n- if ots: check_ots → may raise Bad if file missing? Actually check_ots opens the file; if missing, open raises FileNotFoundError → not caught (only DeserializationError, ValueError, TypeError, EOFError) → generic Exception → exit 3 FAILURE. Hmm, but listdir only returns existing files, so missing files never get here.\n\nBetter fix: derive expected anchors from sigstore anchors' ranges, and check the .ots file for the range head exists (and maybe is present). Also perhaps check that the set of anchor files present is consistent? The task focuses: \"check-anchors must exit 1 when the .ots proof for any attested range head is missing.\"\n\nImplementation: collect ranges during loop (sigstore). Then after loop, for each range (lo, hi, commit), check os.path.exists(os.path.join(a.ledger, \"anchors\", f\"{hi:06d}.ots\")); if not, bad.append(f\"{hi:06d}.ots is missing\").\n\nBut wait — could there be multiple sigstore anchors with same hi? No, one per head presumably.\n\nAlso should we check ots anchors that exist but whose head has no sigstore? Not required.\n\nAlso: should the missing ots be reported even when there are no sigstore bundles? With no anchors at all, the existing test expects \"never attested by the writer\" — the missing check must not break it. With no ranges, no missing-ots checks happen; fine.\n\nEdge: What about the case where sigstore anchor exists for range ending at N, and .ots for N missing → bad: \"000002.ots is missing\" → printed to stderr as \"BAD: 000002.ots is missing\" → contains \"000002.ots is missing\". rc=1. \n\nAlso maybe check ots for the head when the sigstore range is from lo..hi: the proof is for the head entry hi (the new head). Per workflow: `ots stamp` on the new